## air-column

air-column-ask = How much does the air above each square metre of ground weigh?
air-column-title = The Earth holds the air: the pressure up and down
air-column-choices = Next to nothing: air is light
air-column-choices-2 = About a tonne
air-column-choices-3 = About 10 tonnes
air-column-place-label = City
air-column-build-question = Why does the air not fly off into space? The Earth pulls it — and each layer of air presses on the ones beneath. We take a smartphone with a barometer: read the pressure on the ground floor of a block of flats ({ $name }), then on the ninth floor, and then from an airship over the town. How much does the column of air above each square metre of ground weigh?
air-column-setup = A smartphone with a barometer (an app shows the pressure to hundredths of a hectopascal), a block of flats with a lift, a flight in an airship
